      "tool_use_id": "call_00_vQW4Ymy3QLGhVxBs8nqc4001",
      "arguments": {
        "subagent_type": "kernel-profile-analyzer",
        "description": "Profile round 2 fused kernel",
        "prompt": "Profile measured round 2 (round_num=2) of the KernelGen workspace for operator value_selecting_reduction_backward. Use the profile MCP tools with round_num=2 only.\n\nContext for interpretation: round 2 replaced a 2-kernel zeros+scatter implementation with a single fused kernel (out[row,col] = where(col == indices[row], grad[row], 0)) over a contiguous (R x BK) tile with constexpr K, unmasked stores when M % R == 0 and K is a power of two. TARGET_BYTES=32768 per program, MAX_BK=8192.\n\nMeasured (round 2, walltime): geo_mean 1.5502, all 96 workloads pass. Representative per-workload candidate latencies:\n- timing-14 (fp32 8192x8192 out = 268 MB): 0.3175 ms -> ~845 GB/s\n- timing-2 (fp16 8192x8192 = 134 MB) and timing-8 (bf16 8192x8192 = 134 MB): 0.1842 ms -> ~728 GB/s\n- timing-13 (fp32 4096x4096 = 67 MB): 0.1039 ms\n- 12 small/medium workloads all ~0.098-0.108 ms regardless of element count (1 MB .. 64 MB), which looks like a host/launch floor.\n\nWhat I need to know, in priority order:\n1. For the large workloads (timing-14, timing-2/8, timing-13): is the fused kernel limited by store/MOV-OUT bandwidth, by the per-element where/select compute, by the index gather, or by the number of launched programs (8192 programs of 32 KB each)? Report achieved write bandwidth if the profiler exposes it.\n2. For the small workloads: does the collected kernel time approach zero, i.e. is the measured 0.098 ms walltime dominated by host dispatch rather than the kernel itself?\n3. One actionable next experiment (concrete parameter or code change) with expected effect.\n\nDo not change any candidate files. Return your analysis summary directly."
      }
    }
  ],
